\section{Reference index}{


\code{render_docs()} writes a reference index to \code{reference.md}: a page listing every documented topic with a one-line summary. Link to it from the settings file with the \verb{$ALTDOC_REFERENCE} variable.

Each summary is read from the \verb{\\title\{\}} of the topic's \code{.Rd} file, so it is written once in the roxygen2 \verb{@title} and never retyped. The page is rebuilt on every \code{render_docs()} call, so a summary can never drift out of sync with the help page it describes.

By default the index holds a single "All functions" section listing every topic that is not marked \verb{@keywords internal}. To group the topics instead, create \code{altdoc/reference.yml}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{reference:
  - title: Data
    desc: Load and reshape survey data.
    contents:
      - as_pop_data
      - starts_with("read_")
  - title: Modeling
    subtitle: Estimation
    contents:
      - has_concept("estimation")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Each section may set \code{title}, \code{subtitle}, \code{desc}, and \code{contents}. This is the same schema \code{pkgdown} uses for the \verb{reference:} key of \verb{_pkgdown.yml}, so an existing block can be moved over unchanged.

An entry of \code{contents} is either a topic name (or alias), or one of these selectors:
\itemize{
\item \code{starts_with("x")}, \code{ends_with("x")}, \code{contains("x")}: match the start, end, or any part of a topic name or alias.
\item \code{matches("x")}: match a regular expression.
\item \code{has_keyword("x")}, \code{has_concept("x")}, \code{lacks_concepts("x")}: match the \verb{\\keyword\{\}} and \verb{\\concept\{\}} tags. roxygen2 writes \verb{@family} to \verb{\\concept\{\}}.
\item \code{everything()}: every topic.
}

Prefix an entry with \code{-} to remove its matches instead of adding them; when the first entry of a section is a removal, the section starts from every non-internal topic. Selectors skip topics marked \verb{@keywords internal} unless called with \code{internal = TRUE}.

Topics missing from \code{altdoc/reference.yml} are reported at render time and collected in a trailing "Other" section, so they are never silently dropped from the index.

The page's heading is "Package index", the name \code{pkgdown} gives the same page, so a reader arriving from a "Reference" navigation entry sees a heading that says what the page is rather than repeating how they got there. Set a top-level \code{title} key to change it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{title: Function reference
reference:
  - title: Data
    contents:
      - as_pop_data
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A file holding a bare list of sections with no \verb{reference:} key is still accepted, for a block moved over from \code{pkgdown} unchanged, but has nowhere to put \code{title} and so always uses the default.
}

\section{Sidebar labels}{


Every generator labels a man page in the sidebar with its topic name, which says what a topic is called but not what it does. Set \code{sidebar_labels} to \code{name-and-title} to append the topic's \verb{\\title\{\}}, so the summary written once in the roxygen2 \verb{@title} reaches the sidebar as well as the reference index:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{sidebar_labels: name-and-title
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A topic then reads \verb{as_pop_data(): Load a cross-sectional antibody survey data set} rather than \code{as_pop_data}. The name keeps the \verb{()} suffix only when the topic documents something callable, matching how the reference index writes it.

This is opt-in: the default, \code{name}, is what every generator did before, so an existing site's sidebar does not change until its author asks for it.

Titles are truncated to 40 characters so a long one cannot overflow a narrow sidebar. Only the title is shortened, never the topic name, so entries stay scannable by the name a reader is looking for. Set \code{sidebar_label_width} to change the limit:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{sidebar_labels: name-and-title
sidebar_label_width: 60
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A \code{quarto_website} site whose \code{altdoc/quarto_website.yml} uses \verb{$ALTDOC_SIDEBAR_FOLD} gets a navbar button that folds the sidebar away entirely. That button starts the sidebar open; set \code{sidebar_fold} to \code{collapsed} for a site whose pages are better served by the full width, with the sidebar something a reader opens when they want it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{sidebar_fold: collapsed
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Either way the reader's own choice, once made, is remembered and wins over this setting. It applies only to the button's starting state on a site that has one, so setting it without \verb{$ALTDOC_SIDEBAR_FOLD} does nothing.

\code{render_docs()} also writes an \href{https://llmstxt.org/}{\code{llms.txt}} at the site root: an index of the site's public topics and rendered articles, in Markdown, so a coding agent can read the documentation without scraping HTML. Set \code{llms_txt: false} to skip it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yaml">}}\preformatted{llms_txt: false
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Skipping it also removes a copy an earlier render left behind, so the site does not go on serving an index you have opted out of.

\code{title}, \code{reference}, \code{sidebar_labels}, \code{sidebar_label_width}, \code{sidebar_fold}, and \code{llms_txt} are the only top-level keys \code{altdoc/reference.yml} accepts; anything else is an error. So is an unrecognized \code{sidebar_labels} or \code{sidebar_fold} value, an \code{llms_txt} that is not an unquoted \code{true} or \code{false}, and a \code{sidebar_label_width} that is not a whole number of at least 4 --- below 4 leaves no room for the ellipsis, and a fractional width would be silently truncated, so \code{4.5} would quietly behave as \code{4}.

Setting \code{sidebar_label_width} without \code{sidebar_labels: name-and-title} warns, since the width has nothing to apply to on its own.
}

\section{Altdoc preambles}{


When you call \code{render_docs()}, \code{altdoc} will automatically paste the content of one of these three files to the top of a document:
\itemize{
\item \code{altdoc/preamble_vignettes_qmd.yml}
\item \code{altdoc/preamble_vignettes_rmd.yml}
\item \code{altdoc/preamble_man_qmd.yml}
}

The README file uses the vignette preamble.

To preempt this behavior, add your own preamble to the README file or to a vignette.
}

\section{Freeze}{


When working on a package, running \code{render_docs()} to preview changes can be a time-consuming road block. The argument \code{freeze = TRUE} tries to improve the experience by preventing rerendering of files that have not changed since the last time \code{render_docs()} was ran. Note that changes to package internals will not cause a rerender, so rerendering the entire docs can still be necessary.

For non-Quarto formats, this is done by creating a \code{freeze.rds} file in \verb{altdoc/} that is able to determine which documentation files have changed.

For the Quarto format, we rely on the \href{https://quarto.org/docs/projects/code-execution.html#freeze}{Quarto freeze} feature. Freezing a document needs to be set either at a project or per-file level. Freezing a document needs to be set either at a project or per-file level. To do so, add to either \code{quarto_website.yml} or the frontmatter of a file:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode yml">}}\preformatted{execute:
  freeze: auto
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Auto-link for Quarto websites}{


When the \code{code-link} format setting is \code{true} in \code{altdoc/quarto_website.yml} and the \code{downlit} package is installed, \code{altdoc} will use the \code{downlit} package to replace the function names on the package website by links to web-based package documentation. This works for base R libraries and any package published on CRAN.

To allow internal links to functions documented by \code{altdoc}, we need to include links to correct URLs in the \code{altdoc/pkgdown.yml} file. By default, this file is populated with links to the first URL in the \code{DESCRIPTION}. The \code{pkgdown.yml} file is automatically copied to the root of the \verb{docs/} directory so that \code{downlit} can find it at \verb{<url>/pkgdown.yml}.

Importantly, \code{downlit} requires the \code{pkgdown.yml} file to be live on the website to create links. This means that links will generally not be updated when making purely local changes. Also, links may not be updated the first time an \code{altdoc} website is published to the web, since the file needs to be deployed before \code{downlit} can fetch it. Subsequent renders after the first deployment will have correct links.

\code{downlit} treats the documented package the same as any external package, so once it resolves a link to one of that package's own functions, the target is an absolute URL pointing at the production site recorded in \code{pkgdown.yml} (e.g. \verb{https://your.pkg.site/man/some_fn.html}), not a path relative to the page doing the linking. \code{render_docs()} rewrites these absolute self-links into relative paths after rendering, so they keep resolving when the site is served from somewhere other than that production URL -- most commonly a pull-request preview deploy served under its own subpath. Links to other packages' documentation (base R, CRAN) are untouched, since those are meant to point off-site.
}

